// ============================================================================
// Regions — how Burxt frees memory. No garbage collector, no reference counts,
// no borrow checker: a bump pointer, and a mark that says where it stood.
// ============================================================================
//
// THINK OF A TRAY.
//
// When your program builds something at run time — joining Strings, `to_string`,
// `substring`, `read_file`, a growable array — the bytes have to go somewhere. They
// go on a tray. When you are done with everything on it, you tip the whole tray
// into the bin in one motion:
//
//     region name { ... }
//
// Opening one remembers where the bump pointer stood. Closing it puts the pointer
// back, which releases everything built inside, all at once, in O(1) — however
// many allocations happened, and with no traversal, no finalizers and nothing to
// schedule.
//
// YOU NEVER HAVE TO WRITE ONE.
//
// Until v0.0.146 you did: building anything outside a region was a compile error,
// and every program began with a `region` wrapper it never referred to again. That
// requirement is gone. Your program has a tray from the moment it starts, and this
// file has no wrapper around its top level — look at the left margin.
//
// So what is a region FOR now? Releasing early. That is the whole answer, and the
// numbers at the bottom of this file say when you should care.

// ---- You can build anything, anywhere ---------------------------------------
// No region open. All of this used to be a compile error.
let greeting: String = "hello" + ", " + "world";
print(greeting);

let mutable names: [String] = [];
let a: Int = push(names, "ada");
let b: Int = push(names, "grace");
print(len(names));

// A function that builds its answer needs no annotation either. It used to have to
// say `allocates`, meaning "I build in my caller's region" — the compiler works
// that out now, and both compilers agree on it.
function describe(line: Int, byte: Int) -> String {
    return "line " + to_string(line) + ": unexpected byte " + to_string(byte);
}
print(describe(3, 108));

// ---- What a region is for: releasing early ----------------------------------
// Everything built inside is gone at the closing brace. One store to a pointer.
region report {
    let mutable lines: [String] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 1;
    while i <= 3 {
        let entry: String = "line " + to_string(i);
        let n: Int = push(lines, entry);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    print(len(lines));
    print(lines[0]);
    print(lines[2]);
}
// The three Strings and the array are released here. Without the region they would
// live until the program ended — which for three Strings costs nothing, and for a
// loop over a million rows costs everything.

// ---- The number that tells you when to reach for one ------------------------
// A loop building 100,000 Strings, peak resident memory:
//
//     no region                                    5,280 KB
//     region each { ... } around the loop body     1,408 KB
//
// Memory grows LINEARLY without a region, because nothing is released until the
// program exits. The arena is a 1 GB reservation and running out is a named error
// rather than a crash — but a server loop or a batch over a large input will get
// there. Wrap the work each iteration does in a region and the loop becomes
// constant-memory:
//
//     while more_rows() {
//         region row { handle(next_row()); }
//     }
//
// The rule of thumb: a region per unit of work whose results you do not keep.

// ---- `to_string` of a Bool builds nothing -----------------------------------
// It renders to one of two literals, so there is nothing to release. Worth knowing
// only because it shows the rule is about allocation, not about types.
print(to_string(true));

// ---- What the compiler still refuses, and why -------------------------------
//
// One rule survives, and it is the one that matters: a value built inside a region
// cannot leave it, because the region releases at its closing brace.
//
//     function bad() -> String { region r { return "a" + "b"; } }
//     error: cannot return this String: it was built inside a `region` block, which
//            releases at its closing brace, so its storage would not outlive the
//            call. Move the allocation out of the `region` block, or return a
//            scalar summary.
//
// It applies through a NAME as well as directly, which is a distinction that cost a
// use-after-free to learn: `let s = "a" + "b"; return s;` inside a region was
// accepted until v0.0.142 and printed an empty string, because the check asked
// about the expression and a name is not one.
//
// The check also looks INSIDE aggregates — a record holding a built String is
// itself built. That arm was missing for four versions and let a use-after-free
// through, which is why it walks fields and elements now.
//
// Regions do not nest:
//     region a { region b { } }
//     error: `region b` cannot open inside `region a` — nested regions are not
//            available yet. Close the outer one first, or use a single region for
//            both.
